// The household fuel the countryside gathered itself (`game::Switches::household_biomass`,
// `econ::biomass`; docs/trade.pt-BR.md, "O combustível das famílias"): firewood, dung and straw
// that never went through a market. Each number is the share of all households' fuel in 1925;
// the game puts it on the rural households alone (the towns bought their fuel first: in the
// United States wood was 7 % of all energy in 1925 but heated 23 % of the homes in 1940), so a
// country's rural share is this over its rural share of the people (`countries.ron`), at most 1.
// Read only with the switch on.
//
// Sources (read on the page unless marked):
// - China: farm households got 88.7 % (by value) of their fuel and light from the farm itself
//   (straw, stalks, grass, brush, some wood), 80.9 % in the north and 96.8 % in the east-centre,
//   1922–25: J. L. Buck, *Chinese Farm Economy* (1930), pp. 400–402. Taken as the rural share; the
//   towns (about a tenth of the people) bought theirs.
// - India: about 90 % of rural domestic energy from firewood, crop residues and dung around 1900,
//   93 % in 1970–71 (secondary: the energy surveys quoted in the Indian rural energy literature,
//   not read on the page). Taken as the rural share.
// - Japan: wood-based fuel about 80 % of household fuel in the early 1950s (secondary); firewood
//   and charcoal were 14 % of all primary energy in 1920 (Agency for Natural Resources and
//   Energy, *Energy White Paper 2018*, fig. 110-1-1). Taken as 80 % of households in 1925 (an
//   estimate: it can only have been more).
// - United States: 23 % of homes heated mainly by wood in 1940 (US Census of Housing 1940).
// - United Kingdom: no firewood in the English series of 1925, coal 92.7 % of all energy (Warde,
//   *Energy Consumption in England and Wales 1560–2000*, 2007). Netherlands: firewood 2 % of
//   energy in 1900 and none in 1950 (Kander, Malanima & Warde, *Power to the People*, 2013,
//   data).
// - Italy: firewood 37 % of fuel in 1925; Portugal 69 % (same data).
// - Argentina, Brazil, Mexico: Latin America's 64 % of fuel in 1925; Egypt: Africa's 72 %;
//   Poland: Eastern Europe's 42 %; USSR: wood "more than half" of its energy in the early 1920s
//   (secondary). These regional shares are Malanima's world energy database (World Energy
//   Consumption, 1900–1960), which itself assumes the firewood per head of each region outside
//   Western Europe and North America: estimates.
// - NOT READ FROM A SOURCE (estimates): Germany 5 %, France 15 % (10 % of all energy in 1950),
//   Spain 35 % and Sweden 35 % (firewood a quarter to a half of their energy in 1900–1925), and
//   `by_income` for the countries not listed: the regional shares above by the 1925 GDP per
//   head of the scenario (Asia and Africa, Latin America, Eastern Europe, the rest of Western
//   Europe and North America).
(
    // (at most this 1925 GDP per head, share of the households' fuel gathered), ascending; the
    // last band takes everyone richer.
    by_income: [
        (1500, 0.72),
        (3000, 0.64),
        (5000, 0.42),
        (100000, 0.07),
    ],
    households_1925: [
        ("CHN", 0.80),
        ("RAJ", 0.80),
        ("JAP", 0.80),
        ("USA", 0.23),
        ("GBR", 0.0),
        ("NLD", 0.0),
        ("ITA", 0.37),
        ("POR", 0.69),
        ("ARG", 0.64),
        ("BRA", 0.64),
        ("MEX", 0.64),
        ("EGY", 0.72),
        ("POL", 0.42),
        ("SOV", 0.50),
        ("GER", 0.05),
        ("FRA", 0.15),
        ("ESP", 0.35),
        ("SWE", 0.35),
    ],
)
